I've implemented all seven fixes and re-measured. One deviation needs your review: I didn't use your proposed 0.9 and 0.8 thresholds for "the code is still there". The shipped default is 0.97 for both, because 0.9/0.8 lost several real revivals. `npm test` (438 tests), `npx tsc --noEmit`, `npm run build` and `npm run web:build` pass. Everything is committed as `49b6c1a`, ending in "(Kiro CLI)", and not pushed. Both tasks.md files have a "Post-review fixes 4" section.

## Against your labels

| | Fixes 3 | Your 0.9 / 0.8 | **Shipped 0.97 / 0.97** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Real revival rows kept (of 20) | 18 | 12 | **16** |
| False revival rows dropped (of 27) | 4 | 26 | **26** |
| True re-additions found (of 21) | 12 | 11 | **14** |
| Revivals (events) | 44 records | 20 | **32** (35 records) |
| Via Revert | 6 | 6 | **6** |

**Why 0.97 instead of 0.9 / 0.8.** I measured how much of each labelled tombstone was still present (`research/results/label-containment.txt`). The false ones keep 96–100% of the fingerprint after the death. Several real ones are small edits that keep 93–95%: express de129c28 (`--no-exit`), axios 08119634, and the requests 24bcb9ea Revert. At 0.9 / 0.8 those are lost, and so is the 2023 requests chunked re-landing that the README uses as an example.

**Real rows not kept:**
- **e66667e4:** only 10% of the code came back. The fixes-3 miner missed it too.
- **flask 4f03a769:** it has only 3 hashes, so `minHashes` drops it.
- **axios `index.d.cts`:** a second block of an event that is still reported through its `index.d.ts` row.
- **express 1bf2dbb7:** now reported at the earlier commit 3d597a55, which really re-adds those lines.

**False row still kept:** the duplicate patch 64a9c720. Its twin a872f92a (which you labelled ambiguous) is no longer reported, so 64a9c720 is now the only record of that event.

**Revivals you haven't labelled:** 14 records (13 events). My reading is that about 4 are real and the rest are boilerplate (flask `LICENSE`), data (axios `sponsors.json`) and the ambiguous STATUS_CODES table. They need labels before the 32 can be called precise.

## Tombstones and repo counts

- **Tombstone noise:** tombstones whose code was ≥95% still in the touched files after the death fell from 952 (15.9%) to 100 (1.9%). The 100 left are the 0.95–0.97 edits, on purpose. Your 20-tombstone sample isn't in `research/labels/`, so I couldn't re-check it.
- **6 repos:** tombstones 5,970 → 5,169, alive at HEAD 642 → 364, revivals 44 records → 32 events (6 via Revert). This is in research §6, README "Why it matters" and the regenerated web data.
- **Web counts (fix 7):** the stats strip now counts revivals of the tombstones it lists (24 events) and says that counting all tombstones gives 32.

## Identifier-renamed score (fix 5, research §10)

The penalty and floor were picked from the table below: p=0.2, and the renamed score only counts where the raw score of the same window is at least 0.3. A match that only the renaming reaches is a warning, never a block.

| Case | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Rename 2–4 identifiers, reaching warn | 76.0% | 91.4% |
| Rename 2–4 + reorder | 59.3% | 76.4% |
| Unrelated tombstone pairs (false alarms) | 0 of 1,745 | 0 of 1,745 |
| 504 replayed ordinary file writes with an alarm | 5 (8 alarms) | 5 (8 alarms) |

The renamed score adds no alarms. The 8 alarms were already there before this change (similar test cases) and this change doesn't fix them. I couldn't re-run your own 10 rewrites because they aren't in `research/labels/`.

## Costs and remaining misses

- **Index time:** express now takes 12 s instead of 1.7 s, because the miner reads files after each commit and parent trees for revival candidates. That is still well under the 60 s target, and output is still byte-identical across runs.
- **Hook benchmark:** 360–450 ms against its 500 ms budget (about 300 ms before).
- **Recall misses still open:** e5fc88ad and e9f94e69 (windowed scores 0.85 and 0.82). Accepting containment ≥0.9 would find 17 of 21, but it pushed revivals to 60 events, mostly partial matches inside test-suite rewrites, so I left it out.
- **PBT bug found:** `commentSyntax("valueOf")` picked up a function from the object prototype. It's fixed and logged as finding 10 in `research/pbt-findings.md`.

